  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 134 PM CST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nSunday through the Thanksgiving Holiday...overall, no major system \nappears to loom over the holiday travel period. A fairly active \npattern will continue to dominate the central and northern CONUS. \nThe first of multiple disturbances will depart the area on Sunday \nwith subtle height rises expected in its wake. As deeper troughing \nforms between the Great Lakes and Hudson Bay, a weaker system will \ncross the Southern Rockies Monday into early Tuesday and should lead \nto some low-end precipitation chances. The decreasing trend has \ncontinued, with greater disorganization and further south placement \nhas supported decreasing PoPs in western Nebraska for Tuesday. NBM \nprobabilities for measurable snow have falling to less than 10% for \nthe whole of the forecast area with the lone exception of far \nsouthwest Nebraska. This is considerably more pessimistic than the \nwidespread 50-70% for south of Highway 2 from the previous run. This \nisn't an issue of just a warmer forecast eating away at snow as \nEPS/GEFS guidance show bleak potential for seeing even measurable \nmoisture (rain or snow). The inherited forecast does maintain some \nSlight Chance PoPs and the EPS in particular suggests this is fair \nat this point. Though inner-quartile spread remains large, overall \nthe forecast continues to show a gradual warming from Tuesday \nthrough at least Friday. Normal highs for November 26th, which \nhappens to be Thanksgiving this year, are 48F and 46F at North \nPlatte and Valentine respectively. NBM shows about a 38% and 60% \npotential to see above normal temperatures at each respective site \nfor Thanksgiving. These probabilities continue to climb into the \nweekend with the warmest day of the week likely to be Friday or \nSaturday. The upper pattern become quite convoluted by the latter \nhalf of the week and this casts some limited certainty on day-to-day \npredictability. That said, another deep trough appears likely to set \nup somewhere over the upper Midwest by Friday and within a \nnorthwesterly flow regime, the weather should remain generally dry \nthrough the end of the forecast period.",
